not Clojure's `not=` — different name). Actually wait, the primitive is named `!=` (not `not=`), which isn't even a Clojure core function name. So it's a custom primitive. The 0-arity is a non-issue for the review.\n\nActually, I won't flag this — it's a trivial edge case not in scope.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is there a concern that `clj-=`'s 2-arity `([a b] (boolean (data-equiv a b)))` returns a boolean, but the 0/1-arity return `true` (not `(boolean true)`, but that's fine). And `clj-!=` `([a b] (not (clj-= a b)))` → boolean. Fine.\n\nNow, let me also double-check the `clj-=` variadic change handles the `(= 1 1 nil 2)` case correctly with the new code:\n```clojure\n([a b & more]\n (if (data-equiv a b)\n   (loop [prev b xs (seq more)]\n     (if xs\n       (let [nxt (first xs)]\n         (if (data-equiv prev nxt)\n           (recur nxt (next xs))\n           false))\n       true))\n   false))\n```\n\n`(= 1 1 nil 2)`:\n- data-equiv 1 1 → true.\n- loop prev=1, xs=(nil 2).\n- xs truthy → nxt=nil → data-equiv 1 nil → false → return false. ✓.\n\n`(= 1 1 false 1)`:\n- prev=1, xs=(false 1), nxt=false → data-equiv 1 false → false → false. ✓.\n\n`(= false false false)`:\n- data-equiv false false → true (data-equiv on non-numbers? false equals false). prev=false, xs=(false), nxt=false, data-equiv false false → true → recur prev=false xs=() → xs nil → true. ✓.\n\nGood.\n\nNow — one more important verification: the `clj-=` for booleans/nil uses `data-equiv`. Does `data-equiv` correctly handle false/nil (non-numeric values)? The gate4-data-oracle test only checks numbers. But the B-3 test checks `(= false false false)` → true, `(= nil nil nil)` → true, etc., and these pass (green). So `data-equiv` handles non-numeric values. ✓.\n\nAlright. I'm confident the r2 work is correct. Let me write the findings.\n\nLet me also verify one last thing: whether the B-6 syntax-quote for `` `1 `` executes to `1` through the REPL path (the test asserts `(= 1 (repl-session-result \"`1\"))`). This requires the syntax-quote expansion `(quote 1)`? No wait — `` `1 `` → self-evaluating → `1` (the literal 1, NOT `(quote 1)`). So `(repl-session-result \"`1\")` → evaluates `1` → `1`. ✓. And `` `x `` → `(quote x)`. So evaluating `` `x `` → the symbol x (quoted). This is correct Clojure syntax-quote semantics (`` `x `` → `x` the symbol).\n\nHmm wait — actually in Clojure, `` `x `` evaluates to the symbol `x` (because syntax-quote qualifies and quotes it). And `` `1 `` evaluates to `1` (self-evaluating). The test asserts `(= 1 (repl-session-result \"`1\"))`. ✓.\n\nOK. Now let me also make sure the syntax-quote expansion forms (`clojure.core/sequence`, `clojure.core/concat`, etc.) are available in the REPL's primitive set. The test `(repl-session-result \"`[1.0 ~x]\")` only reads (not evaluates), and `(repl-session-result \"(let [x 1.0] `~x)\")` evaluates `` `~x `` → x (the float), which doesn't need sequence/concat. So the complex collection syntax-quote expansion execution isn't fully tested via REPL, but the read-only test `(seq? sq-vec)` confirms the expansion shape. Since the B-6 requirement was \"scalar syntax-quote (backtick 1 -> 1) must execute as before\", and that's tested, this is sufficient.\n\nAlright, writing the findings now.\n\nLet me get the current time.","signature":"c814fb20-56a5-4e6c-b080-550729f2969e"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1541,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":215552,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9f2c1e4a-7b3d-4e8f-a6c5-d0e9f8a1b2c3","uuid":"12e99ec6-fe93-490b-9cfa-e29882b31281","timestamp":"2026-11-26T17:24:09.627Z","thinking_duration_ms":60202,"thinking_display":"updates"}
